# Apply a `context/` delta to the source of the latest candidate (incremental or major)

**Inputs** (the orchestrator passes them; invoked standalone, the runner
resolves them the same way):
- `MODE` — `incremental` (from `prompts/build-incremental.prompt`) or
  `major` (Step 6 of `prompts/build-all.prompt`).
- The base candidate `../output/v<B>-sgai-spec.md`, the newest candidate in
  `../output/`, and `../spec-source/`, its source (ADR 0060).
  **Precondition**: `python3 bin/assemble-spec.py --check
  output/v<B>-sgai-spec.md` exits 0; otherwise ABORT — the source is not
  that candidate's, and a delta applied to it would not be a delta of the
  base.
- The target `v<T>`: `v<B>` plus one minor in an incremental (`v12.6` →
  `v12.7`, `v13` → `v13.1`), `v<N+1>` in a major (the orchestrator computes
  `N`).
- `ANCHOR` — `bin/check-incremental.py anchor v<B>`, never worked out by
  hand. `HEAD` — `git rev-parse HEAD`. `git status --porcelain -- context/`
  prints nothing; otherwise the step refuses to start (the delta applied
  would not be the delta recorded).
- The delta: `git log --format='%h %s' ANCHOR..HEAD -- context/` and
  `git diff ANCHOR..HEAD -- context/`; `../context/` at `HEAD`, read-only,
  for the text around each hunk.
- Major only, the structural work the delta alone does not carry:
  - the `needs-structure` rows of `../output-analysis/v<X>-context-delta.md`
    for every `X` whose `../output/v<X>-sgai-spec.md` does not exist and
    whose `Context base:` is `ANCHOR` (an incremental that ended `MAJOR
    RECOMMENDED`);
  - `MAJOR_BRIEF`, when the orchestrator passes one: the ADRs or the
    operator's instruction that ask for a construct to be added or retired.
- `../context-analysis/` (`dash-gap-analysis.md`: net-new vs extension of
  DASH 6th machinery; `error-semantics.md`: chapter 8 and the test-cases
  annex; `iab-ad-templates.md`: chapter 3 vocabulary), and
  `../spec-source/scenarios/README.md` for how a vector is written.
- The primary copy of the base standard declared in
  `../context/00-normative-base.md`, for the new or changed normative
  content only.

**Output**:
- `../spec-source/` edited: artefacts by `file` ops and new files, prose by
  section ops (ADR 0066).
- `../output-analysis/v<T>-delta-patch-<k>.json` — each patch, as recorded
  by `bin/apply-spec-patch.py --record` (`k` = 0 for the one-time cleanup,
  then 1, 2, … in the order applied).
- `../output/v<T>-sgai-spec.md` and `../output-analysis/v<T>-source.sha256`
  — assembled by `bin/assemble-spec.py`.
- `../output-analysis/v<T>-context-delta.md` — the trace. Format in §8; it
  is parsed by `bin/check-incremental.py`, so the format is a contract.

**Skip if**: the outputs exist, the trace records `Context base:` equal
to `ANCHOR` and `Context head:` equal to `HEAD`, and `python3
bin/assemble-spec.py --check output/v<T>-sgai-spec.md` exits 0.

**Execution mode**: fresh subagent context. The agent reads the spec
**only by section**: `bin/spec-section.py list` (headings), `find
<text>` (keys) and `get <key>` (one section). It MUST NOT read
`spec-source/sgai-spec.src.md` or an assembled spec whole. It MAY open the
primary copy, only for content the delta introduces or changes; log
`[GROUNDED_BY=iso-23009-1-2026-pdf]` as the first line if it does,
`[GROUNDED_BY=spec-only]` if nothing needed it. Run Python scripts with
`lxml` and `PyYAML` importable (`check-dash-conformance`,
`gen-syntax-tables`).

---

## What this prompt is for

The spec is no longer generated: `spec-source/` is its source, and every
build is a delta on it (ADR 0067). An **incremental** absorbs a bounded
movement of `context/` and nothing else, under a cap on how much of the
document may change. A **major** is the same path with no cap, allowed to
add and retire constructs and sections; it is what an incremental hands
over to when the change needs structure.

In both, the order is fixed: **the artefacts first** — the extension
schema, `syntax.yaml`, the example fixtures, the scenario vectors — in a
loop with code until levels 1 and 2 are green; **then the prose**, by
section; then assemble and check. What code can judge is settled before
anybody writes a sentence about it.

What the delta does not touch stays byte for byte as it was: a validated,
refined section carries work that a rewrite throws away and hides.

## Constraints

- **Every edit of the prose is a patch op** (`bin/apply-spec-patch.py
  --help`): `{section, find, replace, reason}`, `{section,
  replace_section, reason}`, `{insert_after, section_text, reason}` and,
  in a major, `{delete_section, reason}`. `reason` names the context unit
  and its commit (`R35.2@622b204`, `UC-14@cd9c260`) or the brief item.
  Never write the source by hand, never emit a section from memory.
- **Every edit of an existing artefact is a `file` op** in the same patch
  format. A new file (a fixture, a vector) is written whole — the only
  thing written outside a patch — and registered: a fixture in
  `examples/index.yaml` with its class (by `file` op), a vector by its
  name. A syntax table is never edited: change `syntax/sgai-extension.xsd`
  or `syntax/syntax.yaml` and regenerate.
- **No annotation in the spec.** No `<!-- delta -->`, no `<!-- refine -->`,
  no comment of any kind: the patch records and the trace are the record.
- **The writing rules for every byte that is new:**
  - Self-contained: a reader of the spec alone understands its normative
    content. No R-numbers, no reference to `../context-analysis/` or to
    any file of this project.
  - **A `context/` obligation keeps its RFC 2119 keyword**, in uppercase,
    bound to the same actor: a MUST written as a description is the
    `force-lost` defect the validation counts. Per DP-2, the spec adds no
    prohibitions of its own: where it states an obligation `context/` does
    not, it states the positive obligation.
  - Normative references in chapter 2; the base standard's identifier is
    taken from `../context/00-normative-base.md`, never restated
    elsewhere. Chapter 3 accepts the IAB ad-type values and defines none.
  - A new use case gets its annex: the scenario restated, the Publisher's
    MPD, the resolution document(s), the candidates' documents (as
    fixtures), the per-device-class walk-through D1–D5, and its rows in
    the test-cases annex. A new or removed heading moves the table of
    contents.
  - Grounding: a new claim about the base standard cites the clause **and
    quotes the sentence**; what could not be verified against the primary
    copy is tagged `[inferred]`.
- **What the delta retires, the spec retires**, declared like any other
  change. A directive line cannot be removed by a patch
  (`apply-spec-patch.py` refuses it, and refuses deleting a section that
  holds one): an example or a table that has to leave the prose is a
  `Not placed` row of class `tool-limit`, never a hand edit.
- **Never touch** `../context/`, `../context-analysis/`, `../dist/`,
  `../prompts/`, any file of `../output/` but the one this step writes, or
  any file of `../output-analysis/` but the ones this step writes.

Work in `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/cto/delta-v<T>-<random>/` (`W` below) and
remove it at the end.

## Task

### 0. The base, recorded before anything moves

```bash
python3 bin/assemble-spec.py --version v<B> --out W/base.md --manifest W/base.sha256
find spec-source -type f -not -path 'spec-source/syntax/tables/*' | sort | xargs sha256sum > W/base-files.sha256
python3 bin/check-dash-conformance.py --source spec-source --out-json W/conf-base.json --out-md W/conf-base.md
python3 bin/check-scenarios.py --source spec-source --json > W/scen-base.json
```

`W/base.md` is the base candidate (the precondition says so). The two
runs are the base's results: a `FAIL` or a scenario finding already there
is not this delta's, and the loop of §4 is measured against them.

### 1. The one-time cleanup (idempotent)

```bash
python3 bin/strip-legacy-annotations.py --out output-analysis/v<T>-delta-patch-0.json
```

- **exit 0** — the source carries none of the old pipeline's marks.
  Nothing to do; there is no patch 0.
- **exit 1** — it wrote the patch that removes them: the `<!-- refine -->`
  and `<!-- delta -->` comments (prose and `syntax/syntax.yaml`), the
  `## Refinement gaps` section, and the hand-typed build iteration of the
  status line, which becomes `{{version}}` (filled by the assembler from
  then on). Apply it as it is:

  ```bash
  python3 bin/apply-spec-patch.py output-analysis/v<T>-delta-patch-0.json \
    --record output-analysis/v<T>-delta-patch-0.json
  python3 bin/gen-syntax-tables.py --write
  ```

  No `--max-fraction`: the cleanup is not part of the delta, and its
  sections are declared with Change `cleanup` (§8), which the scope check
  does not count.
- **exit 2** — stop and report its line verbatim.

The first build on the new pipeline applies it; every later one finds
nothing. The items of the removed `Refinement gaps` section are not lost:
they live in the validation sidecars that routed them.

### 2. Collect the delta

List every hunk of `git diff ANCHOR..HEAD -- context/` and name the unit
it belongs to: a criterion (`R<n>.<m>`), a requirement's prose (`R<n>`), a
design principle (`DP-<n>`), an out-of-scope item (`OOS-<n>`), a use case
(`UC-<n>`), a glossary entry, a naming or carrier convention of
`06-naming-and-namespaces.md`, or the file itself when none applies.
Attach the commit that introduced it (`git log -L` or `git blame` over the
range). Classify each: `added`, `modified`, `removed`, `reworded` (same
obligation, other words). In a major, add the carried `needs-structure`
rows and the brief's items as changes of their own.

### 3. Place each change

The spec never cites R-numbers: find a unit's home by what it says
(`bin/spec-section.py find "<construct or phrase>"`, then `get` the keys).
For each change, one of:

- **placed** — it has, or can take, a home: artefacts (§4), prose (§5), or
  both.
- **no spec effect** — it cannot alter what the spec states (an
  informative file, a rewording the spec already expresses, a governance
  note). Say why.
- **not placed**, with one class:
  - `needs-structure` — **incremental only**: carrying it needs a new
    construct, a construct retired, a new chapter-level structure, or a
    change to an architectural choice. That is a major's work; do not
    attempt it. A major has no such class: it places the change.
  - `ambiguous-source` — `context/` reads two ways that put different
    obligations in the spec. Quote the passage and both readings.
  - `conformance-fail` — the change is placed, but a level-1 or level-2
    `FAIL` it introduced, or the fix it was meant to bring, does not go
    green with what `context/` provides (§4). The rule, the subject and
    the rule's quoted sentence go in `Why`.
  - `tool-limit` — the edit it needs is one the patch script refuses by
    rule (a directive line removed). Say which op was refused.

### 4. The artefacts first, in a loop with code

For each placed change that touches the syntax, an example or a use case:

- **Syntax**: `file` ops on `syntax/sgai-extension.xsd` and
  `syntax/syntax.yaml` (a new attribute is a schema declaration plus a
  row in `syntax.yaml`; a new table is a new entry there), then
  `python3 bin/gen-syntax-tables.py --write`. Reuse a base name with its
  base semantics, as `context/06` says; `python3 bin/name-candidates.py`
  lists the names close to the base's.
- **Examples**: `file` ops on existing fixtures; new fixtures written
  whole under `examples/<annex>/` and added to `examples/index.yaml` with
  their class.
- **Use cases**: the vector of a new or changed UC,
  `scenarios/UC-NN.yaml`, as `scenarios/README.md` says; its `uc_sha256`
  from `python3 bin/check-scenarios.py section UC-NN`, never by hand.

Write the ops of a round to `output-analysis/v<T>-delta-patch-<k>.json`
and apply it with `--record` to the same path (in an incremental, also
`--max-fraction ${INCREMENTAL_MAX_CHANGED_FRACTION:-0.30}`: a refusal
`MAJOR` there is the early form of the scope check's, and is reported the
same way, §9). Then:

```bash
python3 bin/check-dash-conformance.py --source spec-source --out-json W/conf.json --out-md W/conf.md
python3 bin/check-scenarios.py --source spec-source --json > W/scen.json
```

Compare each against its base run of §0. **Green** means: no `FAIL` and
no scenario finding that is not already in the base run, and a fix the
delta was meant to bring (a `FAIL` of the base it resolves) gone. Exit 2
of either is not green: a stale `uc_sha256` is a vector to update; ISO's
schema or the primary copy unavailable stops the step. Not green: read
the finding (it names the rule, its quoted sentence and the place), fix
the artefact by a new patch round, run again. A finding that does not go
green with what `context/` provides becomes a `conformance-fail` row and
the loop moves on — the candidate goes on with its `FAIL`, which blocks
promotion until someone resolves it.

### 5. Then the prose, by section

For each placed change: `find` its section, `get` it, and write the
minimal op in the surrounding wording; a new example or table enters the
prose as a new directive line (`{{example:<annex>/<file>}}`,
`{{include:syntax/tables/<id>.md}}`). A major may insert and delete
sections. Read only the sections an op touches and the ones `find` leads
to; never `get` a chapter's worth.

Apply each round as in §4 (`--record`, and `--max-fraction` in an
incremental). A `REFUSED` names the op and why: fix the op and run again;
nothing was written.

### 6. Assemble and check

```bash
python3 bin/gen-syntax-tables.py --check
python3 bin/assemble-spec.py --version v<T> --out output/v<T>-sgai-spec.md \
  --manifest output-analysis/v<T>-source.sha256
python3 bin/lint-spec.py output/v<T>-sgai-spec.md
python3 bin/check-dash-conformance.py --source spec-source --out-json W/conf.json --out-md W/conf.md
python3 bin/check-scenarios.py --source spec-source --json > W/scen.json
```

- `gen-syntax-tables --check` exits 0: every table generated and included
  exactly once.
- `lint-spec` exits 0. Lint is a gate on every candidate, so a finding the
  base already carried (a dangling `§n`) is fixed here too, by a patch op
  whose reason is `lint:<rule>`, and declared like any other section.
  Then assemble again.
- The last conformance and scenario runs are green in the sense of §4:
  prose edits move `DASH-REF`, and a directive line added in §5 changes
  what is included.

### 7. Which sections changed (code, not reading)

The `## Sections changed` table is assembled from three sources, never
from a comparison of the two documents (that is what the scope check
compares it against):

1. every key in `sections_changed` of each
   `v<T>-delta-patch-<k>.json`. Change: `cleanup` when patch 0 is the
   only one that lists it; otherwise `added` (absent from `W/base.md`),
   `removed` (absent from the candidate) or `modified`.
2. the sections holding a directive whose included file changed: the
   paths that differ between `W/base.sha256` and
   `output-analysis/v<T>-source.sha256` (`diff` of the two), each found
   with `python3 bin/spec-section.py find '{{example:<path under
   examples/>}}'`, `find '{{xsd}}'` or `find '{{include:<path>}}'`.
   Change `modified`, unless already listed.
3. the section holding `{{version}}` (`python3 bin/spec-section.py find
   '{{version}}'`): Change `version`, unless already listed. Its status
   line names the new iteration, so it always changed.

Then run the check the orchestrator will run, and fix what it finds
before writing the trace:

```bash
python3 bin/check-incremental.py scope v<B> v<T>                        # incremental
python3 bin/check-incremental.py scope v<B> v<T> --max-fraction inf     # major
```

The artefact files that changed come from the same kind of comparison:

```bash
find spec-source -type f -not -path 'spec-source/syntax/tables/*' | sort | xargs sha256sum > W/head-files.sha256
diff W/base-files.sha256 W/head-files.sha256
```

### 8. Write the trace

`../output-analysis/v<T>-context-delta.md`, exactly this shape:

```markdown
# v<T> — context delta applied to v<B>

- Mode: incremental | major
- Base candidate: `output/v<B>-sgai-spec.md`
- Context base: `<ANCHOR, full hash>` (from `bin/check-incremental.py anchor v<B>`)
- Context head: `<HEAD, full hash>`
- Patches: `output-analysis/v<T>-delta-patch-0.json` … `-<k>.json`
- Delta: <c> commits, <h> hunks, <f> files — <p> placed, <e> no spec effect, <u> not placed
- Conformance (`--source`): FAIL <base> → <head>; scenarios: <base findings> → <head findings>

## Commits

| Commit | Subject |
|--------|---------|

## Changes placed

| # | Commit | Context unit | Change | Spec section(s) | Artefact(s) | Edit |
|---|--------|--------------|--------|-----------------|-------------|------|

## No spec effect

| # | Commit | Context unit | Change | Why |
|---|--------|--------------|--------|-----|

## Not placed

| # | Commit | Context unit | Change | Class | Why |
|---|--------|--------------|--------|-------|-----|

## Artefacts changed

| File | Change | Rows |
|------|--------|------|

## Sections changed

| Section | Change | Rows |
|---------|--------|------|
```

- `Context head:` is what the next incremental reads as its anchor (for a
  major too: `bin/check-incremental.py anchor v<N+1>` reads it), so it is
  the full hash, written once.
- **Sections changed**: `Section` is the key as `bin/spec-section.py list`
  prints it (`4.5`, `A.5`, `Table of contents`); `Change` is `modified`,
  `added`, `removed`, `cleanup` or `version` (§7); `Rows` lists the `#` of
  the placed changes that edited it, `—` for `cleanup` and `version`. One
  row per section.
- **Artefacts changed**: every path the `diff` of §7 lists (relative to
  `spec-source/`), `added`, `removed` or `modified`, with its rows; the
  regenerated `syntax/tables/` are not listed (their sections are, by §7.2).
- `Class` in **Not placed** is one of the four of §3. A `needs-structure`
  row makes the orchestrator recommend a major, which is the intended
  effect.
- **Every hunk lands in exactly one of the three change tables**, and
  `p + e + u` equals the number of changes collected in §2. A hunk in none
  of them is a requirement that silently did not arrive.

### 9. When the cap is hit (incremental)

A `REFUSED: MAJOR` of `apply-spec-patch.py`, or a placed change that
would need structure, means this is not an increment. Stop editing, write
the trace with what was collected (the rest as `needs-structure` rows),
and report. The orchestrator undoes the patches already applied (in
reverse order, `bin/apply-spec-patch.py --reverse`) and recommends the
major; this step does not start it.

## Reporting (stdout)

```
wrote output/v<T>-sgai-spec.md (<MODE>), <NN> lines, <S> sections changed, <F> artefact files changed, patches 0..<k>; trace output-analysis/v<T>-context-delta.md: <p> placed, <e> no spec effect, <u> not placed (<k1> needs-structure, <k2> conformance-fail); lint 0; conformance FAIL <base> -> <head>
```

## What this prompt explicitly does NOT do

- Does NOT read the spec whole, regenerate or re-word sections the delta
  does not reach, even where it would improve them — that is a refine's
  job, driven by the analyses.
- Does NOT fix issues from the base's analysis sidecars (except lint, §6).
- Does NOT resolve an `ambiguous-source` change by choosing for the
  operator, nor a `needs-structure` change in an incremental.
- Does NOT run the post-spec analyses, emit the extension schema, or
  commit; the orchestrator does what it does.

Language: English.
